--TEST--
Core\Totp names RFC 4226 § 4's floor on both sides — fifteen octets is refused by both members with the count in the sentence, sixteen is accepted — and the refusal is a LogicError rather than a weaker deployment — ADR 0060 §§ 1, 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A bound asserted on both sides, over the one thing about a TOTP deployment
// that using it every day would never reveal: a secret below the RFC's 128-bit
// floor produces codes that verify perfectly and a second factor worth far less
// than it looks. So the floor is enforced rather than documented, and where it
// sits is the whole of the assertion — a check written one octet out passes
// every existing case, all of which ask well below it.
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/totp.rs`'s `secret_at` is the home of the check.

// The refused side, swept and counted rather than sampled: every length under
// the floor throws, including the one immediately under it.
array<uint> $short = [1, 4, 8, 15];
int $refused = 0;
foreach ($short as uint $len) {
    try {
        echo Core\Totp::code(Core\Random::bytes($len)), "\n";
    } catch (LogicError $under) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    }
}

// The accepted side, from the floor itself upward — 16 is the first length that
// works, and nothing above it is treated as a special case either.
array<uint> $long = [16, 17, 20, 32, 64];
int $accepted = 0;
foreach ($long as uint $len) {
    if (Core\Str::length(Core\Totp::code(Core\Random::bytes($len))) == 6) {
        $accepted = $accepted + 1;
    }
}
echo "refused ", $refused, " of ", Core\Arr::count($short),
     ", accepted ", $accepted, " of ", Core\Arr::count($long), "\n";

// The two sides named together, one octet apart. A member comparing `>` where
// it means `>=` refuses the RFC's own recommended floor and prints plausibly
// against the counted halves above; only this pair sees it.
try {
    echo Core\Totp::code(Core\Random::bytes(15)), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $fifteen) {
    echo $fifteen->message, "\n";
}
echo "and at sixteen: ", Core\Str::length(Core\Totp::code(Core\Random::bytes(16))), " digits\n";

// And both members ask, each naming itself: a program that verified through a
// secret it could never have issued a code with would have a second factor that
// only ever refuses, which is indistinguishable from a user with a broken
// phone until somebody reads the code.
try {
    echo Core\Totp::check("000000", Core\Random::bytes(15)), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $onCheck) {
    echo $onCheck->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
refused 4 of 4, accepted 5 of 5
Core\Totp::code(): $secret is 15 octets, and RFC 4226 § 4 asks for at least 16 — Core\Random::bytes(20) answers one of the recommended length.
and at sixteen: 6 digits
Core\Totp::check(): $secret is 15 octets, and RFC 4226 § 4 asks for at least 16 — Core\Random::bytes(20) answers one of the recommended length.
